        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ఇంటికి",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ఇంటికి (iṇṭiki) — \"home\", as in \"going home\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It is ఇల్లు, \"a house\", with the -కి \"to\" ending on it, and the house word changes shape to take it. నేను ఇంటికి (iṇṭiki) వెళ్తాను — \"I am going home\"."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ఇంటికి (iṇṭiki) is native Dravidian: iṇṭi, the form illu, \"house\", takes before an ending, which DEDR 494 lists with Tamil il, \"house\", followed by -ki, \"to\"."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A reason to leave that needs no apology."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
